From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Anatoliy Shulika <shulikaa@icloud.com>
Date: Thu, 6 Aug 2026 12:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: broadcom: archer-c2300-v1: add tp_data

The last 13 MiB of the NAND (0x7300000..0x8000000) is left unmapped by
the current layout. Under the vendor firmware this region holds TP-Link's
factory data - the board's MAC address, the product/region information
and the WiFi PA calibration - as a UBI volume named "tp_data".

Describe it as a read-only partition marked "linux,ubi" so the kernel
attaches it at boot and userspace can read the factory data back. It is
never written by OpenWrt.

Signed-off-by: Anatoliy Shulika <shulikaa@icloud.com>
---
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/broadcom/bcmbca/bcm4906-tplink-archer-c2300-v1.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/broadcom/bcmbca/bcm4906-tplink-archer-c2300-v1.dts
@@ -187,6 +187,54 @@
 			compatible = "brcm,bcm4908-firmware";
 			reg = <0x3a00000 0x3900000>;
 		};
+
+		/*
+		 * Everything past the second firmware partition is left
+		 * unmapped by the stock OpenWrt layout: 0x7300000..0x8000000,
+		 * 13 MiB. Under the vendor firmware this tail holds the CFE
+		 * "data" (4 MiB) and "nvram" (1 MiB) partitions plus "misc2"
+		 * (8 MiB), which is where TP-Link keeps its per-unit factory
+		 * data - tp_data/default-mac (the board's real MAC address),
+		 * tp_data/product-info (model, region) and tp_data/radio_bk
+		 * (WiFi PA calibration).
+		 *
+		 * Mapped read-only so the factory data can be read back and
+		 * cannot be damaged; nothing here is written by OpenWrt.
+		 *
+		 * Layout confirmed by reading the partition on a live device
+		 * and comparing against a dump of the vendor "misc2" mtd taken
+		 * under stock firmware. misc2 is the *first* 8 MiB of this
+		 * region (base 0), so offsets match the stock dump exactly:
+		 *
+		 *   0x4ed038  "radio_bk"      (filename record)
+		 *   0x4f5838  "product-info"  (filename record)
+		 *   0x4f9038  "default-mac"   (filename record)
+		 *   0x50f830  MAC address, 6 raw bytes
+		 *
+		 * The remaining ~5 MiB (CFE "data" and "nvram") reads back as
+		 * erased. Note raw reads of this region log a burst of
+		 * "uncorrectable error" messages from the NAND controller: the
+		 * vendor wrote it with a different ECC scheme than OpenWrt
+		 * uses, so ECC never validates even though the data itself
+		 * comes back intact. Harmless, but noisy - silence the console
+		 * with "dmesg -n 1" before dumping it.
+		 *
+		 * tp_data is a real UBIFS filesystem (confirmed by extracting
+		 * a "misc2" dump with ubi_reader), not a flat blob, so the
+		 * offsets above are NOT stable across units or reflashes -
+		 * UBI's physical block layout depends on each flash's own
+		 * wear-leveling history. "compatible = linux,ubi" below makes
+		 * the kernel auto-attach it at boot (drivers/mtd/ubi/build.c,
+		 * ubi_notify_add()), so /lib/preinit/81_tp_data_nvram
+		 * can mount it read-only and read default-mac/radio_bk by
+		 * filename instead of relying on fragile fixed offsets.
+		 */
+		partition@7300000 {
+			compatible = "linux,ubi";
+			label = "tp_data";
+			reg = <0x7300000 0xd00000>;
+			read-only;
+		};
 	};
 };
 
